course: dictionary
lesson: 1092
title: Brag and boast
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To brag means to talk proudly about yourself, what you have or what you did, in a way that annoys other people. A brag is one such boast. Think of it as showing off with words.
explain: Look for clues around the word. «Cousin Molly bragged about a pony, a laptop AND a trampoline» lists lots of things she has, and then her mam rolled her eyes. Eye-rolling tells you the others were not impressed, so brag is not a kind way of talking.
explain: Bragging can hurt a friend's feelings, even when nobody means it to. «Anya didn't mean to brag; she just didn't realise how different her holidays are.» So when you talk about your good news, it helps to ask about other people's news too.
fact: Brag is a verb (I brag, she bragged, they are bragging) and a noun (a brag).
fact: «Brag» means the same as boast: talking too proudly about yourself.
fact: «Bragging rights» is a phrase meaning the right to boast about winning something.
fact: Friendship stories often show that bragging can make others feel left out or sad.
fact: Geordie (from Newcastle): «canny» means nice, and «mam» means mum.
exercise: «Cousin Molly bragged about a pony, a laptop AND a trampoline.» What does bragged mean here? | Gave away her toys to her cousins | Ran around the playground very fast | *Talked proudly about the things she had | Whispered a secret to her friends
exercise: At recess a kid brags, «My dad's taking me to the Bears game Sunday!» Darnell goes quiet. Why might he go quiet? | He is bored of American football | He has never heard of a game before | He wants to brag about his own game | *His dad used to take him, so hearing this hurts
exercise: «Anya didn't mean to brag; she just didn't realise how different her holidays are.» What does this tell you? | Anya had no holidays at all | *Anya was not trying to show off | Anya was trying hard to show off | Anya was feeling cold
test: «You got a jigsaw and socks, pet.» Why does Molly's mam say this? | To ask Molly to brag even more | To tell Molly about a pony | *To show she was not impressed by Molly's bragging | To give Molly a big prize for being clever
test: In the sentence «It was a brag, not a fact», what kind of word is brag? | An adjective | A question word | *A noun | A verb
exam: What does «bragging rights» mean? | A prize for being the quietest in class | *The right to boast about winning something | A rule about school bags | A kind of sweet from the shop
